---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import RetirementCalculator from '@/components/tools/finance/RetirementCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="retirement-calculator">
  <RetirementCalculator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to use the retirement calculator</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Set your <strong>current age</strong> and the <strong>age you want to stop working</strong>. The
        gap between them has the largest effect on the projection.
      </li>
      <li>
        Enter your <strong>current retirement savings</strong> across all accounts: workplace pension,
        personal pension, 401(k), IRA, ISA and any other money kept for retirement.
      </li>
      <li>
        Enter your <strong>monthly contribution</strong> including any employer match, and set a
        <strong>yearly increase</strong> if your contributions rise with your pay.
      </li>
      <li>
        Choose an <strong>expected return</strong> for your actual asset mix, minus fund fees, and an
        <strong>inflation rate</strong> so the projection can show today's money.
      </li>
      <li>
        Read both numbers: the nominal balance is what the statement will say, and the real balance is
        what it will buy.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Why the real figure matters</h2>
    <p>
      If you are 35 now, a million at 65 is less than a million in today's terms. At 2.5% inflation,
      prices roughly double over 30 years, so that million buys what about 477,000 buys today. The
      calculator shows both figures and the gap, because plans built only on the nominal number
      overestimate how comfortable they are.
    </p>
    <p>
      The real return is <code>(1 + r) / (1 + inflation) − 1</code>. A 7% nominal return with 2.5%
      inflation is about 4.39% real. That is the rate at which your purchasing power compounds.
    </p>

    <h2>What 500 a month builds by 65</h2>
    <p>
      Starting from nothing, 7% nominal return, 2.5% inflation, contributions flat in cash terms.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full min-w-[500px] border-collapse text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Start at age</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">Years saving</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">Balance at 65</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">In today's money</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">Income at 4%</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="tabular-nums">
          <tr><td>25</td><td style="text-align:right">40</td><td style="text-align:right">1,312,407</td><td style="text-align:right">488,780</td><td style="text-align:right">52,496</td></tr>
          <tr><td>30</td><td style="text-align:right">35</td><td style="text-align:right">900,527</td><td style="text-align:right">379,456</td><td style="text-align:right">36,021</td></tr>
          <tr><td>35</td><td style="text-align:right">30</td><td style="text-align:right">609,985</td><td style="text-align:right">290,806</td><td style="text-align:right">24,399</td></tr>
          <tr><td>40</td><td style="text-align:right">25</td><td style="text-align:right">405,036</td><td style="text-align:right">218,473</td><td style="text-align:right">16,201</td></tr>
          <tr><td>45</td><td style="text-align:right">20</td><td style="text-align:right">260,463</td><td style="text-align:right">158,953</td><td style="text-align:right">10,419</td></tr>
          <tr><td>50</td><td style="text-align:right">15</td><td style="text-align:right">158,481</td><td style="text-align:right">109,426</td><td style="text-align:right">6,339</td></tr>
          <tr><td>55</td><td style="text-align:right">10</td><td style="text-align:right">86,542</td><td style="text-align:right">67,607</td><td style="text-align:right">3,462</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      If you start at 25 in place of 35, you contribute twice as much and end with more than twice as
      much: 1.31 million against 610,000. The earliest contributions compound the longest, so the extra
      decade is worth more than the extra money.
    </p>

    <h2>Monthly contribution needed to reach a million by 65</h2>
    <p>At a 7% nominal return, starting from zero:</p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full min-w-[360px] border-collapse text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Start at age</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">Monthly contribution</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">Total contributed</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="tabular-nums">
          <tr><td>25</td><td style="text-align:right">381</td><td style="text-align:right">182,880</td></tr>
          <tr><td>30</td><td style="text-align:right">555</td><td style="text-align:right">233,100</td></tr>
          <tr><td>35</td><td style="text-align:right">820</td><td style="text-align:right">295,200</td></tr>
          <tr><td>40</td><td style="text-align:right">1,234</td><td style="text-align:right">370,200</td></tr>
          <tr><td>45</td><td style="text-align:right">1,920</td><td style="text-align:right">460,800</td></tr>
          <tr><td>50</td><td style="text-align:right">3,155</td><td style="text-align:right">567,900</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>The 4% rule and its limits</h2>
    <p>
      The 4% rule comes from studies of historical US portfolios. Withdraw 4% of the starting balance in
      year one, then increase that amount with inflation each year: the portfolio survived every
      historical 30-year window tested. The calculator uses it to turn a pot into an income. The rate is
      adjustable, because whether 4% is still right is disputed.
    </p>
    <p>
      The rule does not account for your asset mix, fees, tax, longer retirements, non-US markets or
      sequence-of-returns risk. A bad first five years hurts far more than the same bad years later,
      because you sell assets as prices fall. Use 4% to size the pot while you save, not as a withdrawal
      policy for the day you retire.
    </p>

    <h2>What this projection excludes</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>State pensions, social security and any defined-benefit pension income.</li>
      <li>
        Contribution limits and tax relief rules, which differ by country and by account type.
      </li>
      <li>
        Investment fees. Subtract your total ongoing charge from the expected return. A 0.75% fee over 30
        years typically costs a fifth of the final balance.
      </li>
      <li>
        Career breaks and redundancy. Almost nobody contributes without interruption for four decades.
      </li>
      <li>
        Volatility. A steady 7% and an average 7% with real market swings give very different outcomes,
        most of all close to retirement.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
